On 16 September, Kipchoge won the [[2018 Berlin Marathon]] in a time of 2:01:39, breaking the previous world record by 1 minute and 18 seconds (2:02:57 set by fellow countryman Dennis Kimetto at the Berlin Marathon in 2014). It was the greatest improvement in a marathon world record time since 1967.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Robinson |first1=Roger |title=Eliud Kipchoge Crushes Marathon World Record at Berlin Marathon |date=16 September 2018 |url=https://www.runnersworld.com
ews/a23244541/berlin-marathon-world-record/ |publisher=Runner's World |access-date=21 October 2019 |archive-date=31 August 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190831110153/https://www.runnersworld.com
ews/a23244541/berlin-marathon-world-record/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He finished 4:43 min ahead of second-placed fellow Kenyan Amos Kipruto. The world record holder from 2013, Wilson Kipsang of Kenya, came in third at 2:06:48.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.welt.de/sport/article181548434/Berlin-Marathon-2018-Kenianer-Eliud-Kipchoge-knackt-den-Weltrekord.html |title=Berlin-Marathon 2018: Kenianer Eliud Kipchoge knackt den Weltrekord |last=WELT |date=16 September 2018 |work=DIE WELT |language=de |access-date=16 September 2018 |archive-date=19 September 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180919100330/https://www.welt.de/sport/article181548434/Berlin-Marathon-2018-Kenianer-Eliud-Kipchoge-knackt-den-Weltrekord.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.iaaf.org
ews/report/wilson-kipsang-sets-world-record-of-20323-in|title=IAAF: Kipsang sets world record of 2:03:23 at Berlin Marathon|website=iaaf.org|access-date=17 September 2018|archive-date=26 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180326142253/https://www.iaaf.org
ews/report/wilson-kipsang-sets-world-record-of-20323-in|url-status=live}}</ref> It was the 11th world record set at the Berlin Marathon.<ref name="BerlinRecords" />

